glass to me is pretty much crazy cool I  make a collection of very bespoke  and unique glass objects that each have  their own aesthetic from an early age in  high school in Bermuda I was lucky  enough to have a kiln I was able to look  at how organically you can create  something with limited resources you  know you've got mud and something to  ignite it with flames and you know  wallah you've got a sculpture that will  last forever I've looked at woodwork  metalwork digital processes and then  fundamentally having glass as a main  medium I came across glassblowing and  glass sculpting and I instantly had a  full-on just fascination with how  material could be manipulated and so  many different ways well blowing is what  the oldest techniques and in my own  practice I'm able to sustainably make  dozens of pieces from one digital mode  which helps for productivity and there's  no byproducts no waste a lot of my work  begins with sketches and drawings and  then evolving into more of a digital  platform and looking at computerized  edit from that point moving on to more  of a 3d basis and then sculpting and 3d  cutting and forming objects from these  sketches from the beginning of my  practice rationally I was very aware of  the limitations of glass making and how  accessible it is to certain places  around the world and looking at the  cultural imprint behind glass making it  is there I have a strong heart for being  an inspiration to younger people of any  background and any ethnicity is really  really significant for them to be aware  that this occupation that doesn't seem  viable to them because of color of the  skin or their personality you shouldn't  not do it because essentially is is  something that you have a passion for a  lot of the work I do is looking at this  is someone's reality this is what they  see over and over again how do you  breach beyond that reality when I'm  working with glass is a constant battle  between my mental state and my physical  state feels like a film a sense of  suspense and drama and knowing that if  you don't respect what you're doing it  could all go terribly wrong a constant  challenge of rate of sweat heat and fire  I think one of the roles traditional  craft plays within my own practice is  sustainability having a sense of  longevity within the work that I do is  really important to me because it's  touching on the culture and heritage  behind glass making and having the  ability to implement digital base  concepts and designs and really  utilizing a new contemporary style is a  real testament to traditional making  [Music]
 

